fuse: stop asking for security.capability the server already promised to clear

FUSE_HANDLE_KILLPRIV_V2 is a connection-wide promise that the *server* strips
setuid, setgid and file capabilities when a file is written: every write below
carries FUSE_WRITE_KILL_SUIDGID and every truncating open carries
FUSE_OPEN_KILL_SUIDGID. The kernel has delegated the job.

It does it anyway. `fuse_file_write_iter`'s writethrough path — which is every
buffered write on a connection without writeback caching — calls
`file_remove_privs()` unconditionally, and the only shortcut that would make
that cheap is unavailable here: `fuse_change_attributes_common()` clears
S_NOSEC on *every* attribute refresh, so IS_NOSEC() is false for any inode that
has ever been looked up. The result is two `getxattr("security.capability")`
round trips per write, asking a question whose answer the connection has
already been told not to care about.

On a virtio-fs share where a round trip crosses into another address space,
that is not a micro-optimisation. Extracting a 66,000-file package tree makes
one such pair per written file, ten percent of all requests, and every one of
them is answered ENODATA.

So skip it when there is nothing local to do: the connection handles killpriv
and the file carries no setuid or setgid bit. A file that *does* carry one
still takes the original path, so the case where `file_remove_privs()` would
have changed something behaves exactly as before.

--- a/fs/fuse/file.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/file.c
@@ -1430,9 +1430,25 @@
 
 	task_io_account_write(count);
 
-	err = file_remove_privs(file);
-	if (err)
-		goto out;
+	/*
+	 * With FUSE_HANDLE_KILLPRIV_V2 the server strips setuid, setgid and
+	 * file capabilities itself — fuse_send_write_pages() sets
+	 * FUSE_WRITE_KILL_SUIDGID on every write, and fuse_send_open() sets
+	 * FUSE_OPEN_KILL_SUIDGID on a truncating open. Doing it here as well
+	 * is not belt and braces: file_remove_privs() reads
+	 * security.capability, and because fuse_change_attributes_common()
+	 * clears S_NOSEC on every attribute refresh the VFS fast path that
+	 * would normally skip that read never survives a lookup. On a
+	 * virtio-fs share it is two round trips per write, answered ENODATA.
+	 *
+	 * A file that actually carries a setuid or setgid bit still takes the
+	 * original path, so the only behaviour that changes is the question
+	 * nobody was going to act on.
+	 */
+	if (!fc->handle_killpriv_v2 || setattr_should_drop_suidgid(idmap, inode)) {
+		err = file_remove_privs(file);
+		if (err)
+			goto out;
+	}
 
 	err = file_update_time(file);
 	if (err)
